## Description

Refactor typings for `option`, `vector`, `map` and `fixedArray` bcs constructors, so the types are inferred properly when called without explicit type params.

## Test plan

> How did you test the new or updated feature?

Vitest type tests (see `bcs.type-d.ts`)

--

### Some more context

Actually, everything started from the codegen. Noticed that some structs that use options are not properly typed. E.g. `sui::borrow::Referent` codegen was the following:
```ts
export function Referent<T extends BcsType<any>>(...typeParameters: [
    T
]) {
    return bcs.struct('Referent', {
        id: bcs.Address,
        value: bcs.option(typeParameters[0])
    });
}
```

As you might notice, the `T` here is the underlying Struct. But when we call `bcs.option(underlyingStruct)` here, its Input and Type can't be inferred natively (additional `InferBcsType` / `InferBcsInput` are needed), so the resulting struct type ends up being **any** (`{ id: string, value: any }`).

Alternative way to fix that would be to tweak the codegen itself and go with smth like this:
```ts
export function Referent<T extends BcsType<any>>(...typeParameters: [
    T
]) {
    return bcs.struct('Referent', {
        id: bcs.Address,
        value: bcs.option<InferBcsType<T>, InferBcsInput<T>>(typeParameters[0])
    });
}
```
...but imo, makes way more sense in `bcs` itself. Lmk your thoughts!

## Summary

